To turn this mock-up into a colorful website with real jokes, you can use HTML and CSS to structure the layout and add visual elements. You can also use JavaScript to fetch the jokes from an API or a JSON file and display them on the website. Here's a basic outline of the steps you can take:

1. Create an HTML structure for the website, including a `<body>` element with a `<h1>` element for the title "My Joke Website".
2. Add CSS styles to make the website visually appealing, such as font styles, colors, and layout.
3. Use JavaScript to fetch the jokes from an API or a JSON file and store them in variables.
4. Create HTML elements for the jokes, such as `<p>` elements for the text.
5. Use JavaScript to replace the placeholders in the handwritten text with the actual jokes and display them on the website.
6. Add interactivity, such as buttons to reveal the punchlines or a way to share the jokes on social media.

Here's a simple example of what the HTML and JavaScript code might look like:
```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>My Joke Website</title>
    <style>
        body {
            font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
            text-align: center;
        }
        h1 {
            margin-top: 50px;
        }
        p {
            margin: 20px 0;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>My Joke Website</h1>
    <p id="joke1">Isaama, <span id="joke1-placeholder"></span>.</p>
    <p id="joke2">Really dumb joke 2. <span id="joke2-placeholder"></span>.</p>
    <script>
        // Fetch jokes from API or JSON file
        fetch('https://example.com/jokes.json')
            .then(response => response.json())
            .then(data => {
                // Replace placeholders with actual jokes
                const joke1Text = data[0].text;
                const joke1Punchline = data[0].punchline;
                document.getElementById('joke1-placeholder').textContent = joke1Punchline;
                document.getElementById('joke1').textContent = joke1Text;

                const joke2Text = data[1].text;
                const joke2Punchline = data[1].punchline;
                document.getElementById('joke2-placeholder').textContent = joke2Punchline;
                document.getElementById('joke2').textContent = joke2Text;
            });
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```
Note that this is just a basic example and you may need to modify it to fit your specific requirements and the API or JSON file you're using.

To turn this mock-up into a colorful and interactive website, you can use HTML, CSS, and JavaScript. Here's a basic outline of how you can achieve this:

1. HTML structure:
	* Create a container for the joke text and button using a `<div>` element.
	* Inside the `<div>`, add an element for the joke text using a `<p>` element.
	* Create a button using a `<button>` element.
2. CSS styling:
	* Add some styling to make the website visually appealing, such as font styles, colors, and backgrounds.
	* Style the button to have a distinct appearance, like a different color or border.
3. JavaScript interactivity:
	* Add an event listener to the button to detect user interactions, such as clicks.
	* When the button is clicked, retrieve a real joke from an API or a database and replace the joke text inside the `<p>` element.
	* Update the styling of the button to indicate that it has been clicked and the joke has been revealed.

Here's a simple example of how you can structure the HTML and add some basic styling using CSS:
```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>My Joke Website</title>
    <style>
        /* Add some basic styling here */
        button {
            background-color: #4CAF50; /* Green button */
            border: none;
            color: white;
            padding: 10px 20px;
            text-align: center;
            text-decoration: none;
            display: inline-block;
            font-size: 16px;
            margin: 4px 2px;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <div>
        <p id="joke">Push to reveal punchline...</p>
        <button>Reveal punchline</button>
    </div>
    <script>
        // Add JavaScript interactivity here
        const button = document.querySelector('button');
        const jokeText = document.querySelector('#joke');

        button.addEventListener('click', () => {
            // Replace the joke text with a real joke
            const joke = 'A math book was unhappy...'; // Replace with a real joke
            jokeText.innerHTML = joke;

            // Update the button styling
            button.style.backgroundColor = 'gray';
            button.style.color = 'white';
        });
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```
This is just a starting point, and you can expand on it by adding more features, such as animations, loading indicators, or error handling. Remember to replace the example joke with a real joke from a reliable source.
